WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.640
A successful prototype answers a limited question: can this idea work well enough to explore?

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.940
Production software has a harder job.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:14.160
It must continue working for real users, changing requirements, operational failures, security expectations,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.380
and future maintainers who were not present during the original AI conversation.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.400
Prototype code often carries useful shortcuts.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:27.780
It may contain hard-coded values, minimal error handling, weak authentication, no migration strategy,

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.400
limited logging, incomplete accessibility, fragile dependencies, and tests that cover only the happy path.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.420
These weaknesses do not mean the prototype was a failure.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.840
They mean the prototype has not yet been prepared for real use.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.900
The same shortcut that helped answer a design question can become a defect when real users, real data,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.360
and real outages enter the picture.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.740
Before treating a prototype as a product, perform a deliberate hardening pass.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:59.880
Review architecture and ownership boundaries, configuration management, secret handling, input validation,

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:07.620
error behavior, logging, monitoring, backup and recovery, performance limits, accessibility, dependency risk,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.920
deployment, rollback, test coverage, and documentation.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.740
Observability means the system gives operators enough signals, such as logs and metrics,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.200
to understand what is happening in production.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.420
Refactoring is part of that transition.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.480
Generated code may contain duplicated logic, inconsistent naming, unnecessary abstractions,

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.780
or logic placed in the wrong layer.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.520
Refactoring should improve structure while preserving observable behavior.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.260
Tests make this safer because they help confirm that the cleanup did not change the intended result.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.560
Important knowledge should live in the repository, not only in the chat history.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:51.580
Future contributors need setup instructions, architecture decisions, environment requirements, test commands,

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.840
known limitations, and operational procedures.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.900
They should not have to reconstruct the system from a vanished conversation or trust that a generated structure was

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.640
sensible by default.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:06.540
Rapid generation and maintainability are not opposites.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:12.360
Speed is most valuable when the resulting system remains understandable, testable, and safe to change.
